    <span class="mono-label">COMPARE</span>
    <h1>How does a virtual job simulation compare to a real internship?</h1>

    <p class="answer-capsule">They are strong at different things. An internship gives you context, mentorship, a network, and a reference, the things that come from being inside a real company. A virtual job simulation gives you a fast, accessible way to produce evaluated proof of skill without waiting for anyone to hire you. The smartest students do not choose. They use a simulation to build proof, and that proof to win the internship, or to stand in for it.</p>

    <div class="tldr">
      <ul>
        <li>An internship's edge is context and relationships: how decisions get made, a network, and a reference from someone who watched you work.</li>
        <li>A simulation's edge is speed, access, and evaluated output. Anyone can start one today and finish with scored proof.</li>
        <li>The hiring value of an internship is largely the proof it leaves behind, and an internship <a href="https://www.strada.org/reports/talent-disrupted" target="_blank" rel="noopener">cuts underemployment risk by 48.5%</a> (Strada).</li>
        <li>A simulation can reproduce that proof, which is why it is the best substitute when an internship is out of reach.</li>
        <li>Used together, a simulation makes you a stronger internship applicant and a fallback if you do not land one.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <section>
      <h2>What does a real internship give you that a simulation cannot?</h2>
      <p>Be honest that an internship has real advantages, because pretending otherwise helps no one. Inside a company you absorb things that are hard to teach: how priorities actually get set, how teams disagree and resolve it, what good work looks like when a manager is standing over it. You build a network of people who can vouch for you later, and you earn a reference from someone who watched you work for months. A simulation, by design, sits outside that environment. So if you can get a good internship, take it. The experience of being in the room is genuinely valuable, and no focused exercise fully replaces it.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What does a simulation do better?</h2>
      <p>Access and proof. Internships are scarce, competitive, and often gated by exactly the pedigree a student without experience does not have, which means the people who most need the proof are the ones least likely to get the internship. A simulation removes the gate. You can start one today, work on real tasks at your own pace, and finish with something an internship takes a summer to produce: evaluated work in your target role. And because the work is scored against a professional standard, you walk away with the part recruiters weigh most heavily, the evidence, on a timeline you control rather than a recruiter's.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>How do you combine them for maximum signal?</h2>
      <p>Run them in sequence. Start with a simulation to build scored proof in the role you want, because that proof immediately strengthens every internship application you send: you are no longer claiming you can do the work, you are showing it. If the internship comes through, you arrive already capable and use the months inside the company to deepen the signal with context and relationships. If it does not, you are not empty-handed, you have evaluated work that does much of the same job in front of a recruiter. We built zero around this loop. Our Middle East university pilot in Spring 2026 showed how fast students improve through short cycles of doing real work and getting specific feedback, which is the same engine that makes a simulation a useful on-ramp to, or substitute for, an internship.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Which should you choose if you can only do one?</h2>
      <p>If you can land a strong internship, do it, and consider doing a simulation first to get in. If you cannot, do the simulation without hesitation, because a blank summer produces no signal and evaluated work produces the kind recruiters most want. The decision is rarely simulation or internship in the abstract. It is internship if you can get one, simulation either way, because the proof it builds helps in both cases.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="faq">
